# These values must match the enabled Laghu ChromeAnalysisQueue setting.
#
# Supported only by the dedicated Debian or compatible-RPM package service. It
# needs bubblewrap >= 0.8.0, an enabled unprivileged user namespace policy, and
# a distribution Chromium ELF below /usr/lib or /usr/lib64. It is supported on
# Linux x86_64 and aarch64 only. The worker mounts only its dedicated Chromium
# runtime directory, individually discovered ELF dependencies, and fixed
# read-only Fontconfig roots; it uses the DevTools Fetch domain to abort every
# non-job request and verifies every interception reply before publishing. EL9 packages
# intentionally omit this optional worker because their base Bubblewrap is too
# old. Each job gets a fresh user, mount, network, and PID namespace plus an
# AF_UNIX-only seccomp filter; startup fails closed with 78 when the boundary
# or configured Chromium runtime is unavailable. A per-job Chrome/CDP failure
# produces no report and never falls back to a less-isolated browser.
# Do not enable this queue in the combined NGINX or Apache containers: use this
# dedicated service instead.
LAGHU_CHROME_ANALYSIS_QUEUE=/run/laghu/chrome-analysis.queue
LAGHU_CHROME_ANALYSIS_OUTPUT=/var/cache/laghu/chrome-analysis

# Set this to the distribution's actual Chromium ELF, not a shell launcher.
# Debian Bookworm's /usr/bin/chromium is a launcher; use this runtime binary.
# Compatible RPM systems may use a different dedicated /usr/lib*/chromium path.
LAGHU_CHROME_ANALYSIS_BROWSER=/usr/lib/chromium/chromium
